          "The Epiglottis (tongue): is a large, leaf shaped piece of elastic cartilage that is covered with epithelium. The stem of the epiglottis is the tapered inferior portion that is attached to the anterior rim of the thyroid cartilage and hyoid bone. The broad superior leaf portion of the epiglottis is unattached and is free to move up and down like a trap door. During swallowing, the pharynx and larynx rise. Elevation of the pharynx widens it to receive food or drink; elevation of the larynx causes the epiglottis to move down and form a lid over the glottis, closing it off. The glottis consists of a pair of folds of mucous membrane, the vocal folds (true vocal cords) in the larynx, and the space between them called the \u2018rima glottidis\u2019. The closing of the larynx in this way during swallowing routes liquids and foods into the esophagus and keeps them out of the larynx and airways. When small particles of dust, smoke, food, or liquids pass into the larynx, a cough reflex occurs, usually expelling the material."
