        "id": "sec-anat-3-17",
        "title": "Structure of the Esophagus",
        "content": [
          "Anatomical position: The upper esophagus lies at the back of the mediastinum behind the\u00a0trachea, and in front of the\u00a0erector spinae muscles\u00a0and the\u00a0vertebral column.",
          "Size: About 25 cm. long.",
          "Shape: The esophagus (eating gullet) is a collapsible muscular tube.",
          "The esophagus (Figure 5.10) starts at the inferior end of the laryngopharynx, passes through the inferior aspect of the neck, and enters to the mediastinum anterior to the vertebral column. Then it enters to the diaphragm through an opening called the \u2018esophageal hiatus\u2019 and ends in the superior portion of the stomach. The part of the stomach protrudes above the diaphragm through the esophageal hiatus. It is termed as \u2018hiatus hernia\u2019.",
          "Figure 5.10: Structure of the esophagus.",
          "Blood Supply to the Esophagus: The esophagus, with blood supply varying along its course. The cervical parts of the esophagus and the upper esophageal sphincter receive blood from inferior, the parts of the esophagus in the thorax from the\u00a0bronchial arteries\u00a0and branches directly from the\u00a0thoracic aorta, and the lower parts of the esophagus and the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) receive blood from the\u00a0left gastric artery\u00a0and the left\u00a0inferior phrenic artery.\u00a0The venous drainage also differs along the course of the esophagus. The upper and lower parts of the esophagus drain into the\u00a0azygos\u00a0and\u00a0hemiazygos veins, and blood from the middle part drains into the\u00a0left gastric vein. All these veins drain into the\u00a0superior vena cava, with the exception of the left gastric vein, which is a branch of the\u00a0portal vein.\u00a0Lymphatically, the upper third of the esophagus drains into the\u00a0deep cervical lymph nodes, the middle into the superior and posterior mediastinal lymph nodes, and the lower esophagus into the\u00a0gastric\u00a0and\u00a0celiac lymph nodes.",
          "Nerve Supply to the Esophagus: The esophagus is innervated by the vagus nerve and the cervical and thoracic\u00a0sympathetic trunk.\u00a0The vagus nerve has a\u00a0parasympathetic\u00a0function, supplying the muscles of the esophagus and stimulating glandular contraction. Two sets of nerve fibres travel in the vagus nerve to supply the muscles. The upper striated muscle, and upper esophageal sphincter, is supplied by neurons with bodies in the\u00a0nucleus ambiguus, whereas fibres that supply the smooth muscle and lower esophageal sphincter have bodies situated in the\u00a0dorsal motor nucleus.\u00a0The vagus nerve plays the primary role in initiating peristalsis.\u00a0The sympathetic trunk has a\u00a0sympathetic\u00a0function. It may enhance the function of the vagus nerve, increasing peristalsis and glandular activity, and causing sphincter contraction. In addition, sympathetic activation may relax the muscle wall and cause blood vessel constriction.\u00a0Sensation along the esophagus is supplied by both nerves, with gross sensation being passed in the vagus nerve and pain passed up the sympathetic trunk."
        ],
