          "Internal jugular vein: Largest among the jugular vein, is a direct continuation of the sigmoid sinus. It begins at the jugular foramen and ends behind the sternal end of the clavicle by joining the subclavian vein to form the brachiocephalic vein. It drains blood from the brain and superficial part of face and neck. It runs in the neck in the carotid sheath, along with   the internal carotid artery below and the vagus nerve.",
          "Brachiocephalic veins: These are located in the root of neck. Each vein is formed posterior to medial end of the clavicle by the union of internal jugular vein and subclavian veins. These veins have no valves. At level of inferior border of 1st costal cartilage the two brachiocephalic veins unite to form superior vena cava. The left brachiocephalic vein receives the thoracic duct, the largest lymph vessel in the body.",
