        "id": "sec-anat-5-5",
        "title": "Structure of the Thymus",
        "content": [
          "Anatomical position:Thethymusis located(Figure 9.8) behind the sternum between the lungs.",
          "Size:Thicknessof a normal thymus decreased with advancing age from 1.1 cm.for the 6-19 year age group to 0.5 cm. for patients over age 50 years.",
          "Weight: In infants, the thymus is large, with a mass of about 70 gram. After puberty, adipose and areolar connective tissue begin to replace the thymic tissue. By the time a person reaches maturity,the gland has atrophied considerably, and in old age it may weigh only 3 gram.",
          "Shape:Triangularshaped organ.",
          "The thymus produces hormones called \u2018thymosins\u2019 that help to train and develop T lymphocytes during fetal development and childhood. The T lymphocytes produced in the thymus go on to protect the body from pathogens throughout a person\u2019s entire life. The thymus becomes inactive during puberty and is slowly replaced by adipose tissue throughout a person\u2019s life.An enveloping layer of connective tissue holds the two lobes closely together, but a connective tissue capsuleseparates the two. Extensions of the capsule, called \u2018trabeculae\u2019, penetrate inward and divide each lobe into lobules.",
          "Figure 9.8: Thymus.",
          "Blood Supply to the Thymus:Small branches enter the thymus from the inferior thyroid and internal thoracic arteries, and there are corresponding veins. Frequently a rather large thymic vein formed by tributaries from both lobes enters the left brachiocephalic vein.",
          "Nerve Supply tothe Thymus:Sympathetic fibers that enter the thymus are presumably vasomotor."
        ],
