        "id": "subj-anat-7-157",
        "question": "Muscles of the abdominal wall: the abdominal wall is strengthened by four pairs of muscles that run at angles to one another. The external and internal obliques and the transversus abdominis (Figure 3.8) occur laterally, but the fasciae of these muscles pairs meet at the midline of the body, forming a tendinous area called the \u2018linea alba\u2019. The rectus abdominis is a super\ufb01cial medial pair of muscles. All of the muscle pairs of the abdominal wall compress the abdominal cavity and support and protect the organs within the abdominal cavity.",
        "answer": "INC standard: Detail anatomical landmarks, physiological mechanisms, and clinical nursing management for Muscles of the abdominal wall: the abdominal wall is strengthened by four pairs of muscles that run at angles to one another. The external and internal obliques and the transversus abdominis (Figure 3.8) occur laterally, but the fasciae of these muscles pairs meet at the midline of the body, forming a tendinous area called the \u2018linea alba\u2019. The rectus abdominis is a super\ufb01cial medial pair of muscles. All of the muscle pairs of the abdominal wall compress the abdominal cavity and support and protect the organs within the abdominal cavity..",
