      {
        "id": "sec-anat-9-28",
        "title": "Structure of the Breast",
        "content": [
          "Each breast or mammary glands (Figure 8.21) is a hemispheric projection of variable size anterior to the pectoralis major and serratus anterior muscles and attached to them by a layer of fascia composed of dense irregular connective tissue. The lobes subdivide into lobules containing secretory cells. The cells are arranged in clusters around a central duct. The smaller ducts combine to form larger ducts called \u2018lactiferous ducts\u2019 for each lobe. The lactiferous ducts open to the outside at the nipple. The breasts also contain suspensory ligaments that help to support it. Each breast contains a circular pigmented area called an \u2018areola\u2019. The areola contains sebaceous glands to help protect the nipple.",
          "Figure 8.21: Mammary gland within the breast.",
          "The external features of the breast include a nipple surrounded by a pigmented areola, whose coloration may deepen during pregnancy. The areola is typically circular and can vary in size from 25 to 100 mm in diameter. The areolar region is characterized by small, raised areolar glands that secrete lubricating fluid during lactation to protect the nipple. When a baby nurses, or draws milk from the breast, the entire areolar region is taken into the mouth. Breast milk is produced by the mammary glands, which are modified sweat glands. The milk itself exits the breast through the nipple via 15 to 20 lactiferous ducts that open on the surface of the nipple.",
          "These lactiferous ducts each extend to a lactiferous sinus that connects to a glandular lobe within the breast itself that contains groups of milk secreting cells in clusters called \u2018alveoli\u2019. The clusters can change in size depending on the amount of milk in the alveolar lumen. Once milk is made in the alveoli, stimulated myoepithelial cells that surround the alveoli contract to push the milk to the lactiferous sinuses. From here, the baby can draw milk through the lactiferous ducts by suckling. The lobes themselves are surrounded by fat tissue, which determines the size of the breast; breast size differs between individuals and does not affect the amount of milk produced. Supporting the breasts are multiple bands of connective tissue called \u2018suspensory ligaments\u2019 (Figure 8.22) that connect the breast tissue to the dermis of the overlying skin. During lactation, milk moves from the alveoli through the lactiferous ducts to the nipple.",
          "Figure 8.22: Gross structure of breast or mammary gland.",
          "Arterial supply to the Breasts: From thoracic branches of axillary arteries, internal mammary arteries and intercostals arteries.",
          "Venous Drainage of the Breasts: Through an anastomotic circle round the base of the nipple. They drain in to internal thoracic vein, axillary and intercostals veins.",
          "Lymph Drainage of the Breasts: In to axillary lymph nodes, internal mammary nodes and supraclavicular nodes.",
          "Nerve Supply to the Breasts: Breasts are supplied by 4th, 5th and 6th thoracic nerves. The nipple and areola contain touch receptors which are stimulated by suckling. During lactation, impulses from these receptors reach the hypothalamus and secrete hormone oxytocin. This helps in expulsion of milk. The reflex is called \u2018neuroendocrine reflex\u2019."
        ],
        "points": [
          "Figure 8.21: Mammary gland within the breast.",
          "Figure 8.22: Gross structure of breast or mammary gland.",
          "Arterial supply to the Breasts: From thoracic branches of axillary arteries, internal mammary arteries and intercostals arteries.",
          "Venous Drainage of the Breasts: Through an anastomotic circle round the base of the nipple. They drain in to internal thoracic vein, axillary and intercostals veins.",
          "Lymph Drainage of the Breasts: In to axillary lymph nodes, internal mammary nodes and supraclavicular nodes.",
          "Nerve Supply to the Breasts: Breasts are supplied by 4th, 5th and 6th thoracic nerves. The nipple and areola contain touch receptors which are stimulated by suckling. During lactation, impulses from these receptors reach the hypothalamus and secrete hormone oxytocin. This helps in expulsion of milk. The reflex is called \u2018neuroendocrine reflex\u2019."
        ],
        "clinicalPearl": "**Clinical Pearl:** Vaginal problems: A baby girl may be born without a vagina or have the vaginal opening blocked by a layer of cells that are higher up in the vagina.",
        "images": [
          {
            "src": "/images/anatomy/diagram_sec-anat-9-28.svg",
            "caption": "Structure of the Breast • Anatomical Architecture & Landmark Guide",
            "alt": "Structure of the Breast"
          }
        ]
      }
