        "id": "sec-anat-10-6",
        "title": "The Cerebellum",
        "content": [
          "The cerebellum (Figure 10.5) is the second largest portion of the brain and occupies the inferior and posterior aspects of the cranial cavity. It is posterior to the medulla and pons and inferior to the posterior portion of the cerebrum. It is separated from the cerebrum by a deep groove known as the \u2018transverse fissure\u2019 and by an extension of the cranial dura mater called the \u2018tentorium cerebelli\u2019. The cerebellum is shaped somewhat like a butterfly. The central constricted area is the vermis, and the lateral wings or lobes are termed \u2018cerebellar hemispheres\u2019.  Each hemisphere consists of lobes that are separated by deep and distinct fissures. The anterior lobe and posterior lobe govern subconscious movements of skeletal muscles. The flocculonodular lobe on the inferior surface is concerned with the sense of equilibrium. Between the cerebellar hemispheres is a sickle shaped extension of the cranial dura mater, the falx cerebelli. It passes only a short distance between the cerebellar hemispheres and contains the occipital sinus. The superficial layer of the cerebellum, called the \u2018cerebellar cortex\u2019, consists of gray matter in a series of slender, parallel ridges called \u2018folia\u2019. Deep to the gray matter are white matter tracts called \u2018arbor vitae\u2019 that resemble branches of a tree.",
          "1019175196850",
          "Figure 10.5: Cerebellum.",
          "Deep within the white matter are masses of gray matter, the cerebellar nuclei. The cerebellum is attached to the brain stem by three paired cerebellar peduncles.",
          "Inferior cerebellar peduncles connect the cerebellum with the medulla oblongata and the spinal cord.",
          "Middle cerebellar peduncles contain only sensory axons, which conduct input from the Pons into the cerebellum.",
          "Superior cerebellar peduncles contain mostly motor axons that conduct output from the cerebellum into the midbrain."
        ],
